{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792528617799,"sessionID":"ses_ea4f89b40ffeJA5Vf8sjCWqpcq","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_MFTRVrW3W7BHPzMh3rja7298","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\pipeline\\config.json"},"output":"<path>C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\pipeline\\config.json</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\n2:   \"$comment\": \"The daily run. Edited by humans only — the agent never touches pipeline/. Times are wall-clock caps per agent call; a phase is re-called until it marks itself done in journal/<date>/state.json or runs out of calls.\",\n3:   \"agent\": \"random\",\n4:   \"branch\": \"main\",\n5:   \"remote\": \"origin\",\n6:   \"dayTimeoutMin\": 270,\n7:   \"variantsMax\": 3,\n8:   \"limits\": {\n9:     \"$comment\": \"The account's session limit, as the agent CLI reports it (Claude Code streams the five-hour and seven-day windows on every call; Codex reports nothing, so a Codex day runs as before). A call the limit rejects does not count against the phase: the runner keeps the tree as journal/<date>/limited/tree.patch, waits for the window - never longer than waitMaxMin, which a five-hour window always fits and a seven-day one never does - and calls again, at most waitsPerDay times. waitAt: before a call, a five-hour window at or past this is waited out first, so the call is not cut halfway. sevenDayStopAt: preflight refuses the day when the seven-day window is past this, since a cut day could not wait for it. graceSec is added to every wait (the CLI's clock is not this machine's). Waits do not count against dayTimeoutMin, so the scheduler's own cap (ExecutionTimeLimit in schedule/one-a-day.task.xml) has to cover dayTimeoutMin + waitMaxMin.\",\n10:     \"waitAt\": 0.9,\n11:     \"waitMaxMin\": 300,\n12:     \"waitsPerDay\": 2,\n13:     \"graceSec\": 90,\n14:     \"sevenDayStopAt\": 0.95\n15:   },\n16:   \"phases\": [\n17:     { \"id\": \"scout\",    \"prompt\": \"10-scout.md\",    \"output\": \"10-scout.md\",    \"timeoutMin\": 20, \"maxCalls\": 2 },\n18:     { \"id\": \"plan\",     \"prompt\": \"20-plan.md\",     \"output\": \"20-brief.md\",    \"timeoutMin\": 15, \"maxCalls\": 2 },\n19:     { \"id\": \"build\",    \"prompt\": \"30-build.md\",    \"output\": \"30-build.md\",    \"timeoutMin\": 45, \"maxCalls\": 3, \"revisePrompt\": \"35-revise.md\" },\n20:     { \"id\": \"critique\", \"prompt\": \"40-critique.md\", \"output\": \"40-critique.md\", \"timeoutMin\": 15, \"maxCalls\": 2, \"revisePrompt\": \"45-recritique.md\" },\n21:     { \"id\": \"ship\",     \"prompt\": \"50-ship.md\",     \"output\": \"50-ship.md\",     \"timeoutMin\": 30, \"maxCalls\": 2, \"onlyIf\": \"decision=ship\" }\n22:   ],\n23:   \"revise\": {\n24:     \"$comment\": \"The critic's NO SHIP is a review, not the end of the day (lib/revise.mjs). When the critic rejects work that exists - critique done, decision no-ship, at least one variant - the verdict moves aside as journal/<date>/40-critique.r<n>.md, build is called again with phases[].revisePrompt appended (answer the verdict, in the same id and folder, rendered into the next variant letter), and the critic judges again with its own revisePrompt. At most maxRounds rounds a run; 0 turns it off and the first verdict stands, as it did until day 013 (2026-10-02), which was closed 22 minutes into a 270-minute day with the fix named in the critique. Rounds live inside dayTimeoutMin; each build call still has its own cap and budget. A day that ends rejected keeps its tree as journal/<date>/rejected/tree.patch, and `node pipeline/run.mjs --date <date> --from revise` takes it up again (at least one round, same roster slot unless --roster or --model says otherwise).\",\n25:     \"maxRounds\": 2\n26:   },\n27:   \"packs\": {\n28:     \"$comment\": \"Preferred pack vocabulary. The plan phase picks an existing pack when one fits; a new pack needs one sentence of justification and at most one new pack lands per day.\",\n29:     \"vocabulary\": [\"social\", \"dev\", \"music\", \"education\", \"business\", \"science\", \"news\", \"personal\", \"ops\", \"community\", \"sports\", \"events\", \"health\", \"finance\"]\n30:   },\n31:   \"roster\": {\n32:     \"$comment\": \"Who runs today when the runner is called with --agent random (the scheduler is). One slot is drawn per day, weighted; the runner walks the draw order and takes the first CLI that is installed and logged in, so an agent that is not on this machine costs a log line, not the day. --roster <id> pins one slot by hand. avoidRepeat \\\"hard\\\" ranks yesterday agent behind every other one, so with two agents installed the CLI alternates and the weights choose WHICH slot of it runs; \\\"soft\\\" only damps the repeat (repeatPenalty) so the draw stays genuinely random; false forgets yesterday. entries[].adapter is merged over adapters.<agent> for that slot only. The spread is deliberate: the journal is more interesting when the same brief is attempted by a heavyweight one day and a cheap model the next, and a no-ship day from a small model is a real result, not a failure.\",\n33:     \"avoidRepeat\": \"hard\",\n34:     \"repeatPenalty\": { \"agent\": 0.3, \"slot\": 0.1 },\n35:     \"$models\": \"claude: aliases fable|opus|sonnet (+[1m] for the 1M-context variant) and haiku. codex: model slugs from the account, best to cheapest - gpt-6-astra (most capable), gpt-5.6-terra (balanced), gpt-5.6-sol (workhorse), gpt-5.6-luna (fast and affordable), gpt-5.5 (previous generation). Codex reasoning levels: low|medium|high|xhigh|max|ultra.\",\n36:     \"$opencode\": \"2026-10-10 (founder): no Anthropic or OpenAI subscription any more. Every enabled slot rides OpenCode Go through agents/opencode.mjs (model opencode-go/<id>): one subscription ($10 Go / $40 Go Plus a month) across Kimi, GLM, DeepSeek, Qwen, MiMo, MiniMax, Grok and GPT, with per-model dollar limits (five hours = 20%, week = 50% of the monthly cap) that the weighted draw spreads across labs; a hit comes back with no reset time, so the day ends with its tree kept and tomorrow draws another slot. Weights follow the October 2026 agentic-coding boards (Kimi K3 and GLM-5.3 on top) until the journal says otherwise. The claude-* slots sit out (enabled: false), kept for the record like the codex-* ones.\",\n37:     \"$retired\": \"2026-09-30 (founder): every codex-* slot sits out (enabled: false) because OpenAI changed the usage limits on the Codex subscription ($20 Plus). Day 011 drained a fresh five-hour window in 13 minutes - scout, plan and 70 s of build at ultra - and the build failed 3/3 (journal/2026-09-30). Not a verdict on the models: the adapter, the pricing rows and the slots stay; flip enabled back to return one. 2026-10-07 (founder): claude-haiku sits out too - a quality floor, not a limits problem. Both Haiku days shipped thin: day 013 needed an operator note and a second session to finish, and day 018's one-minute plan cut the scout's chess board down to six text fields and a coloured square, which the Haiku critic then passed against its own rubric. A Sonnet day costs about $6 against Haiku's $3; the cheapest slot is now claude-sonnet.\",\n38:     \"entries\": [\n39:       { \"id\": \"go-kimi-k3\",              \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/kimi-k3\",              \"weight\": 4 },\n40:       { \"id\": \"go-glm-5.3\",              \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/glm-5.3\",              \"weight\": 4 },\n41:       { \"id\": \"go-deepseek-v4.1-flash\",  \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash\",  \"weight\": 3 },\n42:       { \"id\": \"go-mimo-v2.6-pro\",        \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/mimo-v2.6-pro\",        \"weight\": 3 },\n43:       { \"id\": \"go-qwen3.8-max\",          \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/qwen3.8-max\",          \"weight\": 3 },\n44:       { \"id\": \"go-minimax-m3\",           \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/minimax-m3\",           \"weight\": 2 },\n45:       { \"id\": \"go-kimi-k2.7-code\",       \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/kimi-k2.7-code\",       \"weight\": 2 },\n46:       { \"id\": \"go-grok-4.7\",             \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/grok-4.7\",             \"weight\": 2 },\n47:       { \"id\": \"go-deepseek-v4-pro\",      \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro\",      \"weight\": 2 },\n48:       { \"id\": \"go-gpt-6-luna\",           \"agent\": \"opencode\", \"model\": \"opencode-go/gpt-6-luna\",           \"weight\": 1 },\n49:       { \"id\": \"claude-opus-ultracode\", \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"opus[1m]\",     \"weight\": 4, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"ultracode\": true, \"effort\": \"xhigh\", \"budgetUsd\": 75 } },\n50:       { \"id\": \"claude-opus\",           \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"opus[1m]\",     \"weight\": 3, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"high\", \"budgetUsd\": 30 } },\n51:       { \"id\": \"claude-fable\",          \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"fable[1m]\",    \"weight\": 3, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"high\", \"budgetUsd\": 30 } },\n52:       { \"id\": \"claude-sonnet\",         \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"sonnet[1m]\",   \"weight\": 2, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"high\", \"budgetUsd\": 15 } },\n53:       { \"id\": \"claude-haiku\",          \"agent\": \"claude\", \"model\": \"haiku\",        \"weight\": 1, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"effort\": \"medium\", \"budgetUsd\": 8 } },\n54:       { \"id\": \"codex-astra-ultra\",     \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-6-astra\",  \"weight\": 4, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"ultra\" } },\n55:       { \"id\": \"codex-astra\",           \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-6-astra\",  \"weight\": 3, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } },\n56:       { \"id\": \"codex-terra\",           \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.6-terra\",\"weight\": 3, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } },\n57:       { \"id\": \"codex-sol\",             \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.6-sol\",  \"weight\": 2, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } },\n58:       { \"id\": \"codex-luna\",            \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.6-luna\", \"weight\": 1, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"medium\" } },\n59:       { \"id\": \"codex-5.5\",             \"agent\": \"codex\",  \"model\": \"gpt-5.5\",      \"weight\": 1, \"enabled\": false, \"adapter\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" } }\n60:     ]\n61:   },\n62:   \"pricing\": {\n63:     \"$comment\": \"USD per MILLION tokens, by model-name substring (first match wins, so a specific name goes above its family), for the cost ESTIMATE on the why-tutorial's timeline page when the agent CLI reports no cost itself (Claude Code reports total_cost_usd; Codex does not). Edit to the list prices of the day and bump pricedAt - the card prints the date; a row with its own pricedAt was checked on that date, the rest on the table's. Claude rows: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing on 2026-09-26; cacheWrite is the 1-hour write (2x input), which is the one Claude Code sessions write. The gpt/codex rows were not re-checked (day 5's audit found gpt-5.6-sol at $4 / $0.40 cached / $20). Missing model = no estimate, tokens only. Open-weight rows (kimi, glm, deepseek, mimo, qwen, minimax) plus grok-4.7 and gpt-6-luna: OpenRouter / OpenCode Zen list prices on 2026-10-10; their cache rates are not published, so cached tokens are charged as input (an over-estimate). Under OpenCode Go the day costs the subscription; this estimate is what it drew against the per-model dollar limits.\",\n64:     \"pricedAt\": \"2026-09-20\",\n65:     \"perMillion\": {\n66:       \"opencode-go